# Spectra — The seeded art

Spectra's art is supplied with the project, under `assets/`. Four PNGs cover the
ship and the three drones, and one particle system covers the burst a destroyed
drone pops with. The build draws each of those elements from the seeded file rather
than from art of its own. Everything else the game shows is drawn in code, and the
last section of this file lists it.

## The four sprites

Each sprite is a single PNG, not a sheet: one file per entity, drawn on a
`SPRITE_SIZE` (`64`) square canvas in straight alpha, so only its drawn pixels are
opaque and each composites cleanly over the dark field. There is no frame data;
these are single stills.

| Sprite | File | What it is | The band it depicts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Fighter | `fighter.png` | The resonator-fighter, hull pointing up | Cyan, ring core |
| Shard | `shard.png` | The fixed-band crystalline drone | Magenta, diamond |
| Flux | `flux.png` | The oscillating drone, caught mid-shimmer | Both bands at once |
| Prism | `prism.png` | The two-band drone, shell around core | Cyan shell, magenta core |

The names are `SPRITES` in the order above.

## How they are drawn

Each entity is drawn from its own sprite, centered on the entity's position and
scaled to that entity's footprint: `SHIP_W` by `SHIP_H` for the ship, `SHARD_SIZE`
for a Shard, `FLUX_SIZE` for a Flux, `PRISM_SIZE` for a Prism with its shell intact,
and `PRISM_CORE_SIZE` for one with only its core left.

The art is pixel art. It is sampled without smoothing, so it stays crisp at every
scale the stage is fitted to and never blurs. Any glow around the ship, a drone, or
a bullet is drawn in code around the sprite rather than baked into it.

## Both bands from one silhouette

The two bands are cyan, whose shape accent is the ring, and magenta, whose accent is
the diamond. The ship, the Shards, the Fluxes, and the Prisms each appear in both
bands over a run, and each sprite is seeded in one band-state only.

The other band-state is the same silhouette carrying the other band's color and the
other band's accent. The hull, the crystal, and the shell form never change; only
the band color and the accent do. Whether the tint is composited over the seeded
PNG at draw time or a per-band copy is baked once at load time is yours; what is
fixed is that the shape on screen is the seeded silhouette and that the band always
reads correctly, never a cyan diamond or a magenta ring.

### The ship

`fighter.png` is the ship tuned to cyan. The magenta ship is the same hull with its
core recolored magenta and the accent swapped to the diamond. The core's band always
agrees with the polarity indicator `specs/ui.md` states. The lives readout may reuse
the fighter sprite at a small size.

### The Shard

`shard.png` is the magenta Shard. A cyan Shard is the same crystal in cyan with the
ring accent.

### The Flux

`flux.png` is the Flux mid-shimmer, showing both bands at once. It is drawn during
the shimmer part of a band window. During the held part the Flux is settled on one
band, and it is drawn as the same body in that band's single color and accent, so a
shimmering Flux and a holding Flux are visibly different. `specs/drones.md` states
which part of the window the Flux is in.

### The Prism

`prism.png` is a cyan shell around a magenta core. The magenta-shell Prism is the
same two-layer construction with the two bands swapped. When the shell is broken,
only the core is drawn: the inner layer alone, at `PRISM_CORE_SIZE`, so a Prism with
its shell intact and one with only its core left are told apart at a glance.

## The drone-burst

`drone-burst.json` is a particle system: a description of emitters, forces, and
per-particle curves on a `BURST_FIELD` (`128`) square field, authored as a one-shot
that bursts at the start and decays to an empty field by the end. It is
`BURST_SYSTEM`.

It is played, not hand-coded and not replaced. The runtime that plays it,
`@clockwyrks/particle-runtime`, is already a dependency of the project. Play it
with the package's pure `ParticleSimulator` and draw the particles the simulation
reports; the package's own types are the authoritative API. Each particle the
simulation reports carries its position on the field, its size, its color, its
opacity, its velocity, and its stretch, and the build composites them additively
over the field so the burst reads as light.

| Rule | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| When | One burst starts in the moment a drone is destroyed, by a bullet or by a discharge wave alike. A Prism struck by bullets starts one when its shell breaks and a second when its core is destroyed; a Prism the discharge wave destroys whole starts exactly one. |
| Where | Centered on the destroyed drone's center. |
| How large | The system's `BURST_FIELD` square is scaled to the popped drone's own footprint, so a Prism's burst is larger than a Shard's. |
| How long | A burst plays for `BURST_DURATION` (`0.7`) seconds and is then gone. Nothing loops or lingers. |
| How many | At most `MAX_BURSTS` (`24`) bursts play at once. |
| How it varies | Each burst scatters at random, so successive bursts in a run scatter differently while the flash, the ring, and the two-band sparks read the same. |

## How they are loaded

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build loads the four sprites and the burst system itself. Every URL it requests
resolves against the page rather than against the origin root, so the produced site
runs unchanged whether it is served from the root of a static host or mounted under
a sub-path. `vite.config.ts` serves the `assets/` tree from the project root in
development and copies the same tree into `dist/assets/` when the site is built, so
each file reaches the produced site under the name this file gives it.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine provides the asset loader, documented in the `engine/`
directory at the root of this repository. It resolves every path under one root,
`assets/`, relative to the page the build is served from, and it fetches and decodes
the file. The build loads each sprite and the burst system through that loader
rather than requesting a URL of its own, writing the path relative to that root:
`shard.png`, `drone-burst.json`.

The produced site serves every seeded file under that root, so a path the loader
resolves reaches the seeded file at whatever base path the site is served from.
{{/if}}

## What is drawn in code

No seeded file covers the following. Each is rendered by the build, and the spec
named beside it fixes its behavior.

| Drawn in code | Fixed by |
| --- | --- |
| Every bullet, the player's and the drones' alike, in its band's color and accent | `specs/ship.md`, `specs/swarm.md` |
| The starfield behind the play field, and the field itself | `specs/field.md` |
| The discharge wave | `specs/resonance.md` |
| The field-wide mark a spectral inversion carries | `specs/ui.md` |
| The HUD, every screen, every menu, every banner, and all text | `specs/ui.md` |
| The debug overlay | `specs/instrumentation.md` |
